# Clown kit — the POLYGON half of the clown (cap, hair tufts, ruff, striped
# tunic, stockings, sleeves, mitts, shoes), authored in WAM against clown.blob.
#
# WHY TWO LANGUAGES FOR ONE CHARACTER: see goblin-kit.wam's header — flesh in
# .blob because blended SDFs are round by construction, clothes in WAM because
# smooth-min rounds every edge a garment needs. For THIS character the polygon
# half is also the only way to own COLOUR: all flesh shares one material and
# the face sheet only darkens, so the red nose shell, the purple hair, the
# striped tunic and the red shoes all live here. Attempt one discovered this
# the hard way — its nose was cream like the skin and the owner's verdict was
# "no red nose reading".
#
# THE SKELETON BELOW MIRRORS characters/clown.blob AND MUST TRACK IT. Two
# conversions apply, neither optional:
#
#   * UNITS. WAM lengths are fractions of `height`; .blob's are metres. The
#     clown's height is 1.00, so every number here IS the .blob metre value —
#     but do not let that fool you into skipping the conversion thought if the
#     height ever changes.
#   * PITCH SIGN ON `down` BONES. WAM's pitch tips an `up` bone forward and a
#     `down` bone BACKWARD; .blob's always carries toward +z. Up bones
#     (spine1, chest, neck, skull) share pitch verbatim; down bones (upperarm,
#     forearm, thigh, shin) NEGATE. Silent either way.
#
# FLESH THIS WRAPS, marched off clown.blob with sdBody bisects at each ring's
# OWN height (full widths; w = 2 x half-width, d = 2 x half-depth). These are
# the numbers the garment margins add to — re-march them if the .blob moves:
#
#   belly    y0.42 w=0.304 d=0.195   y0.44 w=0.314 d=0.193   y0.46 w=0.322 d=0.187
#   waist    y0.50 w=0.261 d=0.163   y0.52 w=0.252 d=0.160
#   head     y0.55 w=0.218 (ball)    y0.57 w=0.272 d=0.236   y0.60 w=0.318
#            y0.70 w=0.424           y0.80 w=0.440           y0.825 w=0.440 d=0.422
#            y0.925 w=0.334 d=0.320
#   (the head rows above are the PRE-1.18 skull. headWidth is now 1.18, so the
#   ball runs ~23% wider in x at every height — the cap band, ruff and tufts
#   below are re-fitted against that wider ball; re-measure before retuning.)
#   thigh    hip ball w=0.092        shaft w=0.072           knee ball w=0.080
#   shin     shaft w=0.060           ankle ball w=0.064
#   arm      shoulder ball w=0.080   upper shaft w=0.052     elbow ball w=0.062
#            fore shaft w=0.046      hand mitt-flesh w=0.072
#
# Garment margins: +12-16 mm on cloth (it HANGS, and smooth-min bulges the
# torso BETWEEN sampled rings — attempt one's hem pushed 53 mm through because
# its ring was sized off the wrong height), +6-8 mm on stockings/sleeves
# (tight), +8 mm on the cap band.
#
# BUILD:  scripts/build-wam-kit.sh clown
#   -> public/assets/lab/clown-kit.gltf, loaded via KITS in webgpu/lab-main.ts.
#
# EXPECTED LINTS, correct to ignore (same three as the goblin kit): this is
# clothes plus a nose shell, not a whole character, so spine1/chest have no
# direct geometry, nothing reaches the ground plane except the shoe soles,
# and skull-side pieces sit far from their own bone's neighbours by design.
model clown-kit
  height 1.00
  # chunky = 8-sided lofts, matching the goblin kit: faceted cloth against
  # smooth raymarched flesh. On a toy the facets also read as stitching.
  style chunky

# Values are one notch brighter than the hexes read off the reference: the
# lab lights the kit with one narrow key and no LOOK-table env (this task may
# not edit kit-overlay.ts), so flat standard materials render darker than the
# same hex would in WAM's own viewer. First render came back mud — and the
# SECOND pass bumped the hexes another notch after the stripes still
# vanished (see the palette block's own note).
palette
  # Second pass on brightness: the FIRST values (one notch over the
  # reference hexes) still rendered as mud under the lab's single narrow
  # key — royal read as navy, the pink stripes as grey-mauve, and the stripe
  # contrast itself nearly vanished. These are two notches over the
  # reference now. If a LOOK-table env ever reaches this kit, revisit
  # downward.
  royal   #555be0
  blue    #6d76f2
  pink    #f2a2e6
  purple  #a878e0
  # Silver-grey, not white: the ruff is the one piece wearing this, and the
  # reference reads it as a metallic frill. Was #d2cec6 (both-notches-bright for
  # the harsh key) which looked white; a touch darker keeps the silver read.
  grey    #c2bcb0
  red     #f04a3e
  # The nose gets its OWN red, brighter and more saturated than the shoes'.
  # A clown nose is the most saturated thing on the character and it was
  # sharing the shoe red, which is deep enough to read maroon under the key.
  nose    #ff2d1c
  gold    #e2b13f
  yellow  #f6cf4d
  white   #f6f1e6

skeleton
  # .blob's `root pelvis at 0.36 len=0.06` is a BONE from crotch to hip line;
  # WAM's root is a bare POINT, so `hips` restores the missing segment and
  # every .blob `parent=pelvis` becomes `parent=hips`. See goblin-kit.wam.
  root pelvis at 0.36
  bone hips   parent=pelvis dir=up len=0.06
  bone spine1 parent=hips   dir=up pitch=2   len=0.075
  bone chest  parent=spine1 dir=up pitch=2   len=0.075
  bone neck   parent=chest  dir=up pitch=-10 len=0.045
  bone skull  parent=neck   dir=up pitch=-4  len=0.34
  mirror
    # down-bone pitches NEGATED from .blob: upperarm -8->8, forearm 20->-20,
    # thigh 8->-8, shin -10->10.
    bone clavicle parent=chest    side=0.030 dir=side  len=0.032
    bone upperarm parent=clavicle dir=down pitch=8   tilt=32 len=0.135
    bone forearm  parent=upperarm dir=down pitch=-20 tilt=-6 len=0.115
    bone hand     parent=forearm  dir=down             len=0.058
    bone thigh    parent=hips     side=0.044 dir=down pitch=-8 tilt=6  len=0.175
    bone shin     parent=thigh    dir=down pitch=10  tilt=-6 len=0.165
    bone foot     parent=shin     dir=fwd len=0.095
  end

parts
  # CAP BAND — the close ring that hugs the crown, OPEN SHELL (cap none).
  #
  # Every piece here is an open shell for the goblin kit's reason: a `cap
  # flat` puts a disc straight through the limb, dead on the bone axis, and
  # the fit test then finds a vertex 5 cm inside the head. Rendered
  # double-sided by kit-overlay.ts.
  #
  # The band is what keeps the cap ON the head while the cone above slumps
  # away behind it. Rings are the head ellipsoid's cross-section at each
  # height PLUS 4 mm: rx(y) = 0.2256 x sqrt(1-(dy/0.235)^2) from headRadius
  # 0.235 x width 0.96, centre y 0.767. A TILTED cut across the forehead was
  # tried to match the reference's diagonal cap edge — ray pitched forward,
  # rings sized along it — and floated a centimetre off the ball: an
  # elliptical ring hugs a sphere only in the sphere's own horizontal
  # sections, so horizontal it is.
  #
  # SITS HIGH, off the brow: band bottom y 0.851, where the sheet's eye
  # line lands at y ~0.74-0.80. First version's band bottom was y 0.809 —
  # 14 mm over the eye line — and from the camera's slight downward pitch
  # the brim ate the eyes whole (rescue render: "a dark visor band across
  # the upper face"). The reference sits the cap well BACK off the brow;
  # high is what this grammar can do. Rings re-marched for the new heights:
  # the ball narrows fast up here, and keeping the old 0.450 bottom ring
  # would float the band 15 mm off the scalp.
  loft capband bone=skull at=0.700 dir=up len=0.075 frame=fwd material=royal
    ring 0.00 w=0.530 d=0.415
    ring 0.50 w=0.484 d=0.378
    ring 1.00 w=0.420 d=0.326
    cap start=none end=none

  # CAP CONE — the floppy blue cone, slumping BACKWARD and to one side.
  #
  # THE OWNER'S EXPLICIT COMPLAINT ABOUT ATTEMPT ONE was that its cap
  # swallowed the face. Two fixes carry the note: the BAND above starts at
  # y 0.851 — clear of the eye line (~y 0.74-0.80), so the whole face stays
  # out from under it — and the cone is a SWEEP rather than a loft, so its
  # tip can DROOP. Segments accumulate world-referenced bends (`back=` tips
  # the tangent toward -z, `down=` toward -y): three back-bends carry the
  # cone up and over itself, two down-bends curl the last third past
  # horizontal, which is what "the tip droops down behind" means in
  # grammar this format actually has. yaw=12 drifts the whole cone to one
  # side, like the reference's asymmetric slump.
  #
  # .blob has no arc capsule yet (bend= was still queued when this was
  # authored — see docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-21-blob-primitive-roadmap.md
  # item 1); a bent sweep is the polygon twin of what that feature will give
  # the flesh, and WAM reports its realized path in lint so the droop is
  # checkable without a render.
  #
  # `cap start=none`: WAM sweeps DEFAULT to a flat start cap — a disc
  # straight through the mount point — which here put a fan dead on the
  # skull-bone axis, 70 mm inside the cranium. Same trap as the goblin
  # kit's `cap flat` note, one syntax earlier.
  # The cone's base ring deliberately SWALLOWS the band's top rim (r 0.205
  # against the band top's 0.170 half-width): an earlier draft sat the cone
  # on the scalp above the band and the render showed a stripe of bare head
  # between the two pieces. Mounted at t=0.86 (y 0.906) so the base emerges
  # from INSIDE the band it overlaps — the band's top rim is y 0.926.
  sweep capcone bone=skull at=0.860 offset=(0,0.006,-0.008) dir=up pitch=-14 yaw=12 material=royal
    seg len=0.038 r=0.235 back=14
    seg len=0.050 r=0.190 back=18
    seg len=0.050 r=0.130 back=20 down=10
    seg len=0.045 r=0.070 down=30
    seg len=0.042 r=0.026 down=44 tip
    cap start=none

  # BADGE — the little yellow spot where the reference wears its star, high
  # on the cap band above the ear. WAM has no star generator (attach kinds
  # are hoof/box/sphere/eye; lofts make tubes), so this is a round badge at
  # the star's position — same concession attempt one made, kept because the
  # colour note is what the silhouette wants and a bad polygon star would
  # read worse than no star. Placed ABOVE the tuft's sweep volume: an earlier
  # offset at cheek height compiled fine and was entirely inside the hair,
  # which WAM's own lint caught.
  attach badge bone=skull kind=sphere at=0.70 offset=(0.245,0.030,0.045) size=0.026 material=yellow

  # HAIR — two wide purple tufts sweeping out from under the cap band,
  # sticking sideways past the cheeks almost like wings. Second-biggest
  # silhouette element after the head itself in the reference, and absent
  # from attempt one entirely.
  #

  # RUFF COLLAR — the grey scallop frill the beads and pom-poms sit ON.
  #
  # The collar is the piece that was missing: only the bells were ever
  # modelled, so they floated in a ring around the neck with nothing holding
  # them. This is a flared loft — a bell of grey cloth from the neck-base
  # (y 0.570, tucked into the body) flaring out to the frill edge (y 0.545,
  # half-width 0.20) just under the chin. The beads ride its outer rim; the
  # pom-poms sit a hair beyond it, at the same height, offset by 22.5 degrees
  # so they break the grey scallop's silhouette the way the reference reads.
  #
  # Sized off the NEW head (headWidth 1.18): the head base at this height
  # (y ~0.55-0.61) runs 0.15-0.21 half-wide, so a frill that peaks at 0.20
  # half-width sits right on the head's underside and the beads just proud of
  # it. The top ring tucks up toward the neck bar (r 0.042) so the whole
  # collar hangs off the skull rather than floating in front of it.
  loft ruffc bone=neck at=0.0 dir=down len=0.055 frame=fwd material=grey
    ring 0.00 w=0.30 d=0.24
    ring 0.55 w=0.49 d=0.40
    ring 1.00 w=0.47 d=0.38
    cap start=none end=none
  # GREY SCALLOP BEADS — the little grey balls, on the collar's outer rim.
  # Circle radius 0.20, at the frill height (y ~0.55). Each bead's inner pole
  # tucks ~20 mm into the collar/head; its outer pole stands proud ~12 mm.
  attach bead1 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(0.200,0.020,0.000)         size=0.030 material=grey
  attach bead2 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(0.141,0.020,0.141)         size=0.030 material=grey
  attach bead3 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(0.000,0.020,0.200)         size=0.030 material=grey
  attach bead4 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(-0.141,0.020,0.141)        size=0.030 material=grey
  attach bead5 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(-0.200,0.020,0.000)        size=0.030 material=grey
  attach bead6 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(-0.141,0.020,-0.141)       size=0.030 material=grey
  attach bead7 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(0.000,0.020,-0.200)        size=0.030 material=grey
  attach bead8 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(0.141,0.020,-0.141)        size=0.030 material=grey
  # POMPOMS — red, on the collar's RIM rather than beyond it.
  #
  # THE FLOATING BUG: these sat on a circle of radius 0.235 while the collar's
  # widest ring was only 0.210 half-width, so every pom hung 25 mm past the
  # cloth with nothing under it — the owner's "biggest issue". The rings above
  # now flare to 0.245 half-width, which is the window that makes them ATTACHED
  # AND still visible: the rim must cover each pom's CENTRE (radius 0.235) so
  # it has cloth under it, without covering its OUTER face (0.235 + 0.024 =
  # 0.259) or the red stops breaking the grey silhouette — which is what
  # clown-kit.test.ts's radial-reach race pins. 0.245 sits between the two.
  #
  # The rim and the pom radius are two independent numbers that can drift apart
  # again. If you move one, re-check the other: rim half-width (ring w / 2)
  # must exceed the pom radius, or they float again.
  #
  # STILL ROUND, NOT POINTED. The ask is a pointed jester frill with a pom on
  # each point. WAM cannot aim a radial fan: `dir=` takes only the six axis
  # directions, and driving eight spike bones by `pin ... tail=` was tried and
  # does NOT aim them — the built rim spanned 225-300 degrees instead of a full
  # circle. A pointed ruff needs either an arbitrary-direction bone in WAM or a
  # different construction (a scalloped loft profile).
  attach pom1 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(0.217,0.010,0.090)          size=0.024 material=red
  attach pom2 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(0.090,0.010,0.217)          size=0.024 material=red
  attach pom3 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(-0.090,0.010,0.217)         size=0.024 material=red
  attach pom4 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(-0.217,0.010,0.090)         size=0.024 material=red
  attach pom5 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(-0.217,0.010,-0.090)        size=0.024 material=red
  attach pom6 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(-0.090,0.010,-0.217)        size=0.024 material=red
  attach pom7 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(0.090,0.010,-0.217)         size=0.024 material=red
  attach pom8 bone=neck kind=sphere at=0.5 offset=(0.217,0.010,-0.090)         size=0.024 material=red

  # TUNIC BODICE — vertical blue/pink stripes flaring from under the head
  # ball to the hip line. frame=fwd so d spans front-back exactly like the
  # measured flesh depths. The top ring disappears INTO the head ball (the
  # ball is wider than the bodice all the way down to y 0.55) — correct:
  # the reference's head sits directly on the garment with no neck between.
  #
  # STRIPES RUN AROUND THE TUBE via material_arc (a constant arc over every
  # ring is a stripe down the length). THE SAMPLING TRAP, diagnosed on
  # attempt one and worth restating: material_arc samples each quad at its
  # ENDING edge angle with INCLUSIVE bounds, and chunky lofts put quad
  # boundaries every 45 degrees — bands authored on the grid (0-45, 90-135,
  # ...) paint EVERY quad one colour and the stripes vanish silently. All
  # four arcs below are offset off the grid by 22 degrees for exactly that
  # reason. Keep them off it.
  loft bodice bone=hips at=0.30 dir=up len=0.1425 frame=fwd material=pink material_arc=blue:22-67,blue:112-157,blue:202-245,blue:292-337
    ring 0.00 w=0.174 d=0.218
    ring 0.33 w=0.178 d=0.216
    ring 0.65 w=0.146 d=0.188
    ring 1.00 w=0.156 d=0.142
    cap start=none end=none

  # TUNIC SKIRT — the bell flare, from the hip line to mid-thigh. It MUST
  # stop near mid-thigh: the hem clears the splayed thighs by ~25 mm at its
  # own lowest ring, and any longer needs to out-flare the stance (the
  # goblin kit's fauld lesson).
  #
  # Ring sizing measured at the hem's OWN height, per the header table —
  # attempt one sized its hem off the crotch ring and the belly pushed
  # 53 mm through it.
  # Arcs are the BODICE's shifted +45 degrees: the skirt runs dir=down where
  # the bodice runs up the chain, and the arc convention mirrors with the
  # path — unshifted, the two met at the hip with blue facing pink and read
  # as two different garments.
  loft skirt bone=hips at=1.0 dir=down len=0.150 frame=fwd material=pink material_arc=blue:67-112,blue:157-202,blue:247-292,blue:337-382
    ring 0.00 w=0.170 d=0.220
    ring 0.50 w=0.235 d=0.280
    ring 1.00 w=0.300 d=0.340
    cap start=none end=none

  mirror
    # HAIR — two wide purple tufts sweeping out from under the cap band,
    # sticking sideways past the cheeks almost like wings; second-biggest
    # silhouette element after the head itself in the reference, and absent
    # from attempt one entirely. Authored ONCE here, inside the mirror block:
    # the first draft sat outside it and produced ONE wing (the render caught
    # what the compile lint did not).
    #
    # Mounted just INSIDE the cheek surface (offset x 0.205 against a local
    # half-width of 0.223 — buried 18 mm, attached), aimed dir=side and swept
    # OUT-AND-UP. First version bent down=12/24/36 across its segments and
    # the render read it as drooping dog-ears; the reference's tufts are
    # WINGS — they leave the hairline travelling sideways and climb as they
    # go, so every bend here is up= (WAM sweeps bend up=/down=/fwd=/back=
    # per segment; there is no outward= because dir=side already IS out).
    # The ROOT SEGMENT IS DELIBERATELY THIN (30 mm) and now LONGER (80 mm):
    # a fat ring around an axis below the skin always has vertices deep
    # inside the skull — the fit test rejected a 75 mm-rooted first draft at
    # −71 mm — so the tuft sprouts from a narrow root at the hairline and
    # only swells once clear of the ball. The long thin root also FIXES the
    # "floating flaps" read of the up-swept version: with the swell starting
    # 80 mm out, the first stretch of the wing overlaps the cheek visually
    # and the tuft reads as GROWING from the head, not hovering beside it.
    # `cap start=none` for the same reason as the cap cone above (sweeps
    # default to a flat start cap: a disc straight through the mount point).
    sweep tuft bone=skull at=0.66 offset=(0.202,-0.005,-0.018) dir=side yaw=-12 material=purple
      seg len=0.040 r=0.055 up=4
      seg len=0.055 r=0.078 down=24 back=4
      seg len=0.060 r=0.070 down=34 back=3
      seg len=0.060 r=0.050 down=30
      seg len=0.055 r=0.024 down=16 tip
      cap start=none

    # THE RED NOSE SHELL — the colour half of the clown's one saturated note.
    # The flesh bead underneath carries the silhouette (see clown.blob); this
    # red ball carries the READ. First continuation version was w=0.104
    # d=0.034 — a flat PANCAKE floating in front of the bead, and the render
    # verdict was "no ball". A clown nose is a BALL stuck on the face, so the
    # shell is near-spherical now (h set explicitly — an attach sphere's h
    # DEFAULTS TO size=, which left the first ball version squashed 2:1
    # vertically on the turntable; a clown nose is round in FRONT view too)
    # and centred to straddle the bead: face surface at this height is z≈0.194 (semi-depth 0.212 at
    # dy −0.045, bone z −0.0185), shell back pole z 0.170 buries 24 mm into
    # the skull (inside the fit test's tuck bound), shell front pole z 0.262
    # stands ~36 mm proud of the flesh bead's own front (0.226) — about 70%
    # of the ball in front of the face, which is exactly how a foam clown
    # nose sits.
    attach nose bone=skull kind=sphere at=0.45 offset=(0,-0.045,0.216) size=0.052 w=0.108 h=0.100 d=0.096 material=nose

    # STOCKING — thin blue leg, tight (+6-8 mm over the measured balls and
    # shafts), ending just above the ankle where the shoe takes over.
    loft stocking bones=thigh..shin material=blue
      ring 0.08 w=0.098
      ring 0.35 w=0.086
      ring 0.55 w=0.092
      ring 0.75 w=0.074
      ring 0.93 w=0.072
      cap start=none end=none

    # SLEEVE — thin blue arm tube, shoulder to wrist. The shoulder ring
    # wraps the shoulder ball (w 0.090 over flesh 0.080) so the sleeve
    # reads as dressed from the joint, not glued on halfway.
    loft sleeve bones=upperarm..forearm material=blue
      ring 0.05 w=0.090
      ring 0.30 w=0.064
      ring 0.55 w=0.070
      ring 0.80 w=0.058
      ring 0.95 w=0.054
      cap start=none end=none

    # MITT — the white puff. A sphere on the hand bone, sized to swallow
    # the flesh mitten (r 0.036) whole: the glove is one soft shape, no
    # fingers, exactly like the reference. It rides at the skirt wall —
    # cloth contact is unavoidable at chibi proportions and the reference
    # shows the same brush.
    attach mitt bone=hand kind=sphere at=0.60 offset=(0,0,0.005) size=0.044 material=white

    # SHOE — big, bulbous, RED. Lofted along the foot ray CONTINUED (shin
    # at=1.0, dir=fwd, longer than the foot bone itself): the foot's toe
    # blob rides past its bone's tail, so a shoe ending at t=1.0 of the
    # foot bone stops short of the toe it exists to exaggerate — the
    # goblin kit's boot lesson verbatim. Oversized IS the joke: 0.15 forward
    # of the ankle plus domes, against a 0.095 foot bone.
    # DROPPED 21 mm and fattened. (31 mm overshot: fattening the rings extends
    # the shoe downward too, so the two drops compound and it sank 10 mm
    # THROUGH the floor.) Removing the gold sole took the shoe's
    # lowest geometry with it and WAM's lint caught the result — "lowest
    # geometry floats 0.031 above ground". The shoe now reaches the floor on
    # its own, and since it is the only footwear left it carries the bulk the
    # sole used to: these are meant to be BIG red clown shoes.
    loft shoe bone=shin at=1.0 dir=fwd len=0.165 frame=up offset=(0,-0.021,0) material=red
      ring 0.00 w=0.108 d=0.132
      ring 0.30 w=0.138 d=0.140
      ring 0.60 w=0.148 d=0.124
      ring 0.90 w=0.116 d=0.094
      ring 1.00 w=0.076 d=0.062
      cap start=dome end=dome

    # NO SOLE. There was a gold box lofted under the shoe here as a sole. In the
    # render it did not read as a sole — it read as a SANDAL worn under the shoe,
    # a separate gold slab with its own silhouette below the red. Owner's verdict
    # was "remove the sandles". The shoe is one red mass now.

    # NO TOE BALL. There was a small yellow ball on each toe. Fattening the
    # shoe to reach the ground swallowed it — WAM's lint: "part 'toe.l' is
    # entirely inside 'shoe.l' ... costs triangles and shows nothing". The
    # brief is "just big red clown shoes", so it goes rather than being
    # grown back out through the leather.
  end

textures
  # Cloth stays matte and quiet — heavy noise would muddy the stripe edges
  # the material_arc work exists to keep crisp. The striped materials take
  # the least of anything.
  texture royal base=#555be0
    gradient axis=v from=-0.10 to=0.16
    noise scale=0.14 amount=0.05
    ao amount=0.14
  texture blue base=#6d76f2
    noise scale=0.16 amount=0.04
    ao amount=0.08
  texture pink base=#f2a2e6
    noise scale=0.16 amount=0.04
    ao amount=0.08
  texture purple base=#a878e0
    streaks along=v amount=0.08
    noise scale=0.14 amount=0.05
    ao amount=0.12
  texture grey base=#c2bcb0
    noise scale=0.14 amount=0.05
    ao amount=0.16
  texture red base=#f04a3e
    gradient axis=v from=-0.08 to=0.14
    noise scale=0.14 amount=0.05
    ao amount=0.14
  texture gold base=#e2b13f
    gradient axis=v from=-0.10 to=0.22
